            <div xml:id="d689">

                <pb facs="0715" n="617" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg617"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/1637: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-03T17:00:00+08:00">June 3, 1932—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 3—5:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>601. Legation’s 570, May 27, noon. Following from American Consul General at
                    Nanking.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“June 2, 4 p.m. In conversation June 1, 5 p.m., Vice Minister Hsu Mo said
                        that the Ministry was anxious to receive a reply from the Department
                        indicating whether the postponement of the indemnity <pb facs="0716" n="618"
                            xml:id="pg618"/> could now be considered settled and in this connection
                        he referred to the views expressed to me in previous interviews (see my
                        telegram May 25, 6 p.m.).”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
